+# Native audio/video recorder integration
+
+`NativeAudioVideoRecorder.vue` uses the Vue 2 adapter from the public
+[discuss-it-recorder](https://github.com/johnnyrobot/discuss-it-recorder)
+repository, release v0.1.8. Its recorder code is byte-identical to v0.1.7;
+v0.1.8 adds only this patch and its install guide. These files are copied
+unchanged from the release tag:
+
+- `resources/js/components/recording/VideoRecorder.vue`
+- `resources/js/media/recording/` (`Recorder.js`, `BackgroundProcessor.js`,
+  `BackgroundImage.js`, `segmenter-worker.js`)
+- `resources/vendor/discuss-it/` (the pinned segmentation model, its Apache
+  license, source and checksum)
+- `scripts/build-discuss-it-assets.cjs`
+
+The recorder's MIT notice is in that repository's LICENSE. MediaPipe
+(`@mediapipe/tasks-vision`) is pinned to exactly 0.10.35 under the recorder's
+no-telemetry decision; do not upgrade it to 1.x.
+
+## What the host does
+
+The adapter owns capture, effects, review, retake, download and media cleanup.
+The host uploads only after **Submit recording**. A failed upload keeps the
+take available for retry. The existing pre-signed URL POST fields and parent
+save events are kept for discussion comments, audio submissions and submitted
+work. The filename extension and PUT Content-Type come from the actual
+recording (including Safari MP4/M4A). No backend or storage changes are needed.
+
+- A lost camera or microphone with no recorded data returns to idle with the
+  recorder's disconnect error; nothing is submitted. With recorded data, the
+  partial clip is kept for review and the recorder explains what happened.
+- If the browser never finishes after Stop, a three-second watchdog abandons
+  the take, releases capture and shows "Recording could not be finished.
+  Please record again." If the browser's MediaRecorder reports an error, the
+  take ends the same way. Abandoned takes are never offered for review,
+  download or upload.
+
+`DiscussItViewer.vue` uses this component for new, reply and edit video and
+for mobile audio. Its countdown follows the adapter's `recording` event,
+excluding preview and review. A retake resets the countdown and the length
+requirement. Desktop audio still uses the existing separate audio recorder.
+
+## Where the recorder loads its runtime
+
+`NativeAudioVideoRecorder.vue` passes `asset-base` to `VideoRecorder` as
+`process.env.ASSET_PATH + 'assets/discuss-it/'`. `ASSET_PATH` is defined in
+`webpack.mix.js` as `ASSET_URL + '/'`, or `/` when `ASSET_URL` is unset: the
+same build-time value that already serves the other compiled assets. With
+`ASSET_URL` set, the worker, WASM and model load from that origin; the
+recorder starts the worker through a same-origin `blob:` script, because
+browsers start workers only from the page's own origin. Without it they load
+from `/assets/discuss-it/` on the app's own origin.
+
+`npm run development` and `npm run production` first run
+`npm run build:recorder-assets`, which stages the MediaPipe runtime, the
+worker, the WASM and the checksum-verified model in
+`public/assets/discuss-it/` (ignored by Git). Deploy that directory with
+`public/dist/`. When it is served from another origin, that origin must:
+
+- send `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` (the app's origin, or `*`) on every file
+  under `assets/discuss-it/`;
+- serve `.wasm` files as `application/wasm`;
+- be allowed by any Content-Security-Policy: the asset origin in `script-src`
+  and `connect-src`, `'wasm-unsafe-eval'` in `script-src`, and `blob:` in
+  `worker-src` (or `child-src`/`script-src`, which it falls back to).
+
+Without CORS, effects fail with "Background effects could not start…" and the
+camera keeps working with None.
+
+## Checks
+
+Use Node 22.2 or newer, the version the recorder supports.
+
+```sh
+npx playwright install chromium webkit
+npm run build:recorder-assets
+npm run test:recorder
+npm run production
+```
+
+`npm run test:recorder` mounts the real host component, adapter and recorder
+with ADAPT's Vue 2, synthetic capture and a loopback upload receiver. It reads
+the staged `public/assets/discuss-it/`, so stage the assets first. It runs 22
+cases in each of Chromium and WebKit:
+
+- 3 upload cases, one per upload mode: review before upload, retake, capture
+  release, failed upload and retry, MIME type and extension, and the parent
+  save event;
+- 6 device-loss cases: camera, microphone in video mode and microphone in
+  audio-only mode, each with and without recorded data;
+- 6 watchdog cases: the finish events never arrive after Stop or after device
+  loss, in each mode;
+- 6 MediaRecorder error cases: during the take or while finishing, in each
+  mode;
+- 1 cross-origin case: the staged assets are served from a second local
+  origin that sends `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`, and Blur must start through
+  the `blob:` worker bootstrap.
+
+It uses no accounts, database, ADAPT server, real capture or object storage.
+`PLAYWRIGHT_MODULE` can point at an existing Playwright `index.mjs`.
+Real-device testing and authenticated end-to-end submission and playback are
+separate acceptance checks.
